# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# hangar — environment
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in what your machine actually needs.
# EVERY VARIABLE HERE IS OPTIONAL. A console with an empty .env.local runs
# fine against local services on localhost; the variables below only matter
# once you reach services that are somewhere else, or behind something.
#
# .env.local is gitignored. Nothing in this file is.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Which machine is this? ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Normally leave this unset. The host profile is resolved automatically at
# config load: HOST_ID env → a hostname matching config/hosts/<id>.json → a Mac
# is "b5" → everything else is "betenshi" (see next.config.ts).
#
# Set it to force a profile — useful for previewing another machine's console
# from this one, and the only way a Linux box or a container can pick
# something other than the default.
#
# HOST_ID=b5


# ── Public hostnames for a tunnelled host ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only needed if this machine publishes its services off-box (e.g. behind a
# Cloudflare tunnel) AND you run the console somewhere that must reach them
# over the internet rather than over localhost.
#
# Host profiles write publicUrl as https://<service>.${PUBLIC_DOMAIN} instead
# of a literal hostname, because the full list is a map of what a machine
# exposes and what runs behind each entry. Unset, it resolves to example.com,
# which fails obviously instead of plausibly.
#
# Local use never touches this: getServiceUrl() returns localUrl whenever
# VERCEL is unset, so a console running on the same box as its services
# ignores publicUrl entirely.
#
# PUBLIC_DOMAIN=your-domain.example


# ── Cloudflare Access service token ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Required ONLY to call a service whose host profile says "authRequired": true,
# AND only from a deployed console (VERCEL is set). Running locally, the
# console goes straight to localhost and never presents these.
#
# What they are: a Cloudflare Access *service token* — the machine-to-machine
# credential for an Access-protected hostname. Cloudflare Zero Trust →
# Access → Service Auth → Service Tokens → Create. You get a Client ID and a
# Client Secret; the secret is shown once. The token must also be allowed by
# the Access policy on each protected hostname, or it authenticates and is
# still refused.
#
# The console sends them as CF-Access-Client-Id / CF-Access-Client-Secret
# (src/lib/services.ts → getServiceHeaders).
#
# WHAT BREAKS WITHOUT THEM: nothing at all locally. From a deployed console,
# Cloudflare intercepts the request before it reaches your machine and answers
# with a redirect to the Access login page. That failure is quiet rather than
# loud, and it is worth knowing the exact shape of it:
#
#   fetch() follows the redirect, so the login page arrives as HTTP 200.
#   /api/health only asks whether res.ok — so the service is reported UP,
#   with a green dot and a plausible latency, while every actual call to it
#   gets a page of HTML where it expected JSON.
#
# So the symptom is not "the service is down". It is a service that looks
# perfectly healthy and whose responses will not parse.
#
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=<uuid>.access
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=<64-char hex>


# ── Storage index / database locations ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both default to a per-user application-data directory and are created on
# first use. Override only to move them onto another drive.
#
# HANGAR_DB_PATH=/path/to/betenshi.db
# HANGAR_STORAGE_DB=/path/to/storage-index.sqlite
